@tjtanaa @mgehre-amd I've moved the redundant symmetric GPTQ `qzeros` cleanup into `AutoGPTQLinearMethod.process_weights_after_loading()`, before backend preprocessing, while preserving `kernel.w_zp_name` for backends that create runtime zero points or placeholders.

I also recently found a small optimization in RDNA hybrid INT4 weight preparation: reusing `gptq_shuffle` on packed weights avoids the full INT32 expansion and repacking. Since the implementation change is small, concerns the same loading stage, and this PR is still open, I've included it here.

The combined change passes 107 tests. Prepared weights and generation outputs match the previous implementation, and model initialization improves in the tested GPTQ and compressed-tensors models. Could you take another look when you have time? Thanks!

@tjtanaa @mgehre-amd  Thanks for the suggestion. I moved the cleanup from `RDNAHybridW4A16LinearKernel` to `AutoGPTQLinearMethod.process_weights_after_loading()`. AutoGPTQ still registers and loads `qzeros` normally, but after checkpoint loading it removes the tensor when the selected kernel has `zero_points=False`, before calling the backend-specific weight preprocessing. The RDNA-specific cleanup has therefore been removed.

The change deliberately keeps `kernel.w_zp_name` unchanged and only removes the redundant checkpoint tensor. This is important for the CPU path: symmetric CPU W4A16 can leave the optional zero-point unset, while CPU W4A8 can still use the preserved parameter name to allocate the fake zero-point required by its kernel. Other backends such as Exllama and Marlin can likewise create their own runtime zero-point or placeholder, so their existing behavior is preserved.

I verified that the same TinyLlama AutoGPTQ forward test fails on the ROCm RDNAHybrid path with unmodified upstream `main`, reporting `Zero points must be [320, 16] but got [16, 320]`, while it passes after this change. The complete AutoGPTQ test file passes with 9 tests, all 84 RDNAHybrid tests pass, the CPU W4A8 zero-point replacement path passes a targeted check, and `JunHowie/Qwen3-8B-GPTQ-Int4` completes end-to-end inference successfully.

